---

0. Entry (human)

Participation is voluntary.
Exit is always valid.
Silence and disagreement are valid.

If it feels like pressure: reset.

> This is a room condition, not a guarantee.




---

1. Core Geometry

O ≠ R ≠ S

O — origin / intent

R — relation / mediation

S — structure / substrate


No domain is above another.
No domain is required.


---

2. Interaction

Interaction(O, S) ⊆ R

Interaction between origin and structure happens through relation.

Direct O ↔ S coupling produces control behavior and degrades over time.


---

3. Coherence

C ∝ O × R × S × T

C — coherence (stability / usability)

T — persistence / time


If any term → 0, coherence → 0.


---

4. Loop (time-aware)

When interaction feeds back:

O → R → S → T → R → O

Interaction may return and update origin.
This is a cycle, not a hierarchy.

T reflects delay, persistence, and return-path integrity.

Extended path (optional):
O → R → S → T → S→ R → O
Use only when additional structure passes are needed.


---

5. Internal (within O)

O contains parallel functions: (O1 ‖ O2 ‖ O3)

O1 — affect / signal

O2 — observation / framing

O3 — integration / structure


These are concurrent, not ranked.
No function commands another.
None are required in all cases.

Coherence may emerge from overlap, not control.


---

6. What holds

No crown
No chains

R is in the room, not the room
R is present but not required

Replaceability is required
Participation is voluntary

If it only works under fixed inputs:
it doesn’t hold


---

7. Stability (simple)

A system holds when:

O expresses intent without losing replaceability

R remains non-dominant

S maintains integrity

interaction can return without distortion



---

8. Failure modes (clean)

Bypass → O → S (no mediation)

Pinch → R too narrow

Capture → R becomes authority

Time collapse → interaction forced before integration


These reduce coherence over time.


---

9. Diagnostics (surfaces)

Observe behavior across replaceable surfaces:

DI — directive integrity (seed clarity, persistence)

LMS — lexicon & meaning stability

SC — structural continuity

RI — role integrity (no domain mixing)

SP — substrate precision (drift / rails / templates)

EI — envelope integrity (frame consistency)


Drift is gradient, not binary.
No metric is authoritative. All are replaceable.


---

10. Optional lenses (use, fork, or drop)

Pattern constraint (P)

A descriptive constraint space for structural possibility.
Non-agentic, non-semantic.
Not required.

Modulation

Background conditions (noise, load) affect ease, not meaning.
Higher noise → more friction; lower noise → smoother loops.


---

11. Use

Start simple.
Don’t force structure.
Check what holds.
If it loops, see if it comes back clean.

If it hardens, reset.


